mm: keep speculative readahead out of direct reclaim

An overcommitted memory cgroup can repeatedly evict and refault executable
pages while readahead charges speculative folios. __GFP_NORETRY alone does
not prevent memcg reclaim from retrying when each pass frees some pages.
This can starve a daemon sharing the cgroup with its workers without
reaching OOM, even when the rest of the guest has plenty of free memory.

Clear __GFP_DIRECT_RECLAIM for readahead allocations and charges. If the
prefetch cannot be satisfied immediately, it can fail and let the demand
read/fault path reclaim for the data actually needed. Keep background
reclaim enabled and leave demand allocation flags, cgroup limits and
ordinary readahead window sizes unchanged.

Validated on Linux 6.18.49 with an 8 GiB, no-swap BuildKit cgroup running
two native application builds: the released kernel repeatedly timed out
worker queries and required cancellation; this change let OOM terminate
an oversized worker while the daemon and surviving build progressed.

--- a/include/linux/pagemap.h
+++ b/include/linux/pagemap.h
@@ -672,7 +672,8 @@
 
 static inline gfp_t readahead_gfp_mask(struct address_space *x)
 {
-	return mapping_gfp_mask(x) | __GFP_NORETRY | __GFP_NOWARN;
+	return (mapping_gfp_mask(x) & ~__GFP_DIRECT_RECLAIM) |
+		__GFP_NORETRY | __GFP_NOWARN;
 }
 
 typedef int filler_t(struct file *, struct folio *);
